At last! I *think* I've ascended! Sadly, there is a bit of controversy
over the whole matter, so let's get that out of the way first...

This was easily the best game I'd played - never been past the castle
before. I was prepared reasonably well, I thought, and took the plunge
into the planes, when Rodney turned up, as expected. A few (6?)
careless turns later, I had dispatched Rodney and a clone he had made,
but found myself stressed - he'd gone and cursed my blessed bag of
holding not once but twice. Not knowing the full consequence of this, I
reached into it to extract some holy water, only to have *all* my holy
water vanish (*now* I understand the importance of keeping some
separate. How do others manage this problem + fire traps at the same
time?). Still not fully realising why my stuff had vanished, I
neglected to take anything out of my bag. I went on to roam the plane
of earth for a while, hoping that some nice creature would drop me a
potion of holy water, or scroll of remove curse or something. Nothing.
Tried praying. No joy. Dispassionate, I abandoned my lovely Valk for
the night, to give me time to consider what my best move would be. I'd
prepared a big post addressed to this newsgroup to explain the
situation and ask advice (how should I have prevented this? what should
I do now?). I did this on my office computer (certainly not on company
time... nosiree!). Then, when I got home, I found my computer had
rebooted itself (a very annoying 'feature' in Windows XP sometimes does
an automatic update, then reboots the PC automatically, without any
confirmation or anything... which I think is *incredibly* rude. Anyone
with the same problem can read a solution at
http://blogs.msdn.com/tim_rains/archive/2004/11/15/257877.aspx). Now
the thing is, I had not saved my game last night, so I wondered what
would happen when I restarted. The game told me it had found files
relating to a game in progress, and asked if I would like to restore
them. I counted my blessings simply that I could restore the game, but
when I saw where it was restored, I couldn't quite believe it - right
at the start of plane of earth. I went on to ascend, but the question
is... is this a moral ascension? I don't know how much I could have
salvaged from the bag, and I don't know what kind of chance it would've
given me on the other planes. In my defence, when I did go on to
ascend, I didn't use much from the BoH; several wands of teleport,
mainly, and I suspect I could still have retrieved several, but I don't
really know :-(.

So, if you count it, here is my YAFAP:

I was playing a Lawful Dwarf Valk called Andy (she had very masculine
features, ok?). Andy got off to a good start by successfully pulling of
the protection racket. This was despite reaching experience level 3 by
minetown (due to pet separation and a stubborn unwillingness to just
give up on it). Fortunately, minetown contained numerous shops, and
shopkeepers with plenty of money, and Andy had rejoined her large cat
and had a magic whistle with which to assist in some credit cloning
activity. She thus got to naked AC 1 (or was it 0? Not sure now).

Andy made some stupid mistakes on her onward journey. The worst was
carelessly obliterating her bag of holding (via wand of cancellation -
doh!), which contained numerous potions destined for alchemy, a
plethora of wands, scrolls and so forth. Worst of all, it contained all
her candles. Polypiling and searching the dungeons yielded 3 candles,
but eventually she had to wish for candles.

She had a very nasty moment with a mind flayer. She thought she had one
more turn than she did to use ranged attacks, and ended up down to
intelligence of 4 or so before she knew it. She teleported the flayer
away, and escaped back upstairs. As the had the orb of fate by now, she
resolved not to return to that level until she had done something to
defend against flayers. She did this by doing some polypiling and
getting some scrolls of genocide (subsequently blessed).

She also, I think, 'over-polypiled' in the hope of getting some nice
wands / tools, resulting in many potentially useful items disappearing
(she particularly had a yearning for lembas wafers, and managed to
polypile somewhere in the region of 60 or 70 food items down to a mere
10 lembas). I think she also probably undervalued wands of teleport at
the time, and thus passed several over. Having been to the planes now,
it's not a mistake she'll make in any future reincarnations.

The final big mistake was on her quest. A giant had thrown a boulder
onto the very spot she had dropped her beloved tinning kit, and another
boulder right next to it, in such a way as to make them unmovable. And
she so wanted some tinned giant corpses. 'No matter', she thought,
'I'll just zap them with this here wand of striking'. She was sadly
unaware of the fact that she was standing on, or very close to, a
drawbridge. Her amulet of life saving glowed...

Other than that, I must confess Andy seemed to be blessed with nothing
but good luck. Many stairs were close to each other, aiding the
ascension. Furthermore, the mysterious force didn't hold her back much,
and Rodney only turned up once on the way up, if I recall correctly.
Some nurse dancing, foocubus consorting and alchemy managed to raise
her hit points to a rather mighty 700 or so before the ascension. The
planes themselves presented little problems, despite her fears (she had
read *a lot* about the dungeon before entering). She was never touched
by air elementals, eels and the like had been genocided long before,
levitation + speed enabled her to stroll through all of them, hardly
being touched. Until The astral plane, of course. Then she had her only
dose of bad luck. She was very nervous about meeting the riders. First
of all, she noted where each of them were, then headed in the direction
of famine. Once she saw famine, she zapped him with her wand of death;
but alas, it was only then that she noticed the sickle... Death was
standing right where famine had been! And she'd just healed him. Ah
well. She had to face death at close combat, and had 50 odd max HP
drained before dispatching him. Then she really did have to deal with
famine, but that presented no problems from a distance using a wand of
death. She proceeded to the first altar... chaotic. So she headed out
to the middle altar, as there appeared to be no riders lurking there.
Made it without too many problems, teleporting all and sundry out of
the way... neutral. So she continued on her way toward pestilence, who
again put up little resistance to the wands of death. She made it to
the altar, held the amulet up high above her head, knelt on the altar
with her eyes closed, and her head lowered, and the gods took her...


